# 229: §6 `.35` orders the `Call` steps (c) check arity BEFORE (d) check depth,
# and this case is the only way to tell. At depth 1000 — where a well-formed
# call to a user function is refused, per 226 — a call with the WRONG arity
# reports the arity error, because step (c) runs first and step (d) is never
# reached. An engine that checked depth first would report the recursion limit,
# and both answers look defensible until the order is pinned.
#
# `down(0)` is invocation #1000 at depth 1000; `two(1)` at line 3 has its one
# argument evaluated (a) and `two` resolved (b) successfully, then fails (c).
--- source
fn two(a, b) { return a + b; }
fn down(n) {
  if n == 0 { return two(1); }
  return down(n - 1);
}
print down(999);
--- expect
error: Type at line 3: two expects 2 arguments, got 1
